-- chem/ring.cub — the electron on a ring, exactly. The hopping part of the Dirac term multiplies by holonomies,
-- which act on the gravitational state by multiplication, so it is a direct integral over connections and the
-- electron's spectral measure is the average, over the state's distribution of the ring's holonomy U, of the ring
-- with the fixed twist U (docs/chemical-identity.md, "The electron's paths and the geometry's record of them").
-- The state: a ring whose vertices carry no other coupling, a loop of spin j around it, and the electron at p and
-- the proton at q each an open spin-½ end, so the two arcs from p to q carry j and j − ½. Gauge-fixing the arc
-- of spin j − ½ to 1 leaves U on the other; at p and q the two arcs couple to ½ by the Clebsch–Gordan coefficient
-- ⟨j m, j−½ A−m | ½ A⟩, so in U's eigenbasis (eigenvalues e^{±iφ}) the state is diagonal with entries
-- ψ_A(φ) = Σ_m ⟨j m, j−½ A−m | ½ A⟩² e^{2imφ}. With doubled J = 2j and t = j + m (A = +½),
--   ⟨j m, j−½ ½−m | ½ ½⟩² = 2t / (J(J+1)),   t = 1, …, J,
-- a ramp. Haar measure on the class angle is (1/π) sin²φ dφ on [0, 2π), whose Fourier coefficients are 1 at 0
-- and −½ at ±2. A circuit wound w times multiplies the sector A by e^{2iAwφ}, so the coherence of w circuits is
--   ĉ_w = Σ_A ∫ (1/π) sin²φ |ψ_A|² e^{2iAwφ} dφ / Σ_A ∫ (1/π) sin²φ |ψ_A|² dφ,
-- and with P(d) = Σ_{t'−t = d} t t' (t, t' in 1..J) it is ĉ_{2u} = (2P(u) − P(u+1) − P(u−1)) / (2P(0) − 2P(1)),
-- zero at odd w. The law: ĉ_{2u} = −u / (J(J+1)) for 1 ≤ u ≤ J and 0 beyond, proved from P(d) = G(d) =
-- (J−d)(J+1−d)(2J+1+d)/6 = [d³ − (3J²+3J+1)d + J(J+1)(2J+1)]/6 for d ≤ J, whose second difference is d, and
-- G(0) − G(1) = J(J+1)/2; checked below against the direct sums and the weights against Racah's formula.
import "bholonomy.hypercubical"
import "spin.hypercubical"

